 ### What a thrown value is
 
-JavaScript throws any value. Adamic, for now, throws only an `Error`: `throw new Error(message)`, or the error a `catch` caught, thrown again. Anything else is refused, with the fix `throw new Error(String(value))`. The reason is the catch side: what a `catch` binds is `unknown`, and holding any value there means a value of every kind at once, which 0.2 doesn't have yet. An `Error` is an object like any other, with two fields, `name` and `message`, counted like any object; `stack` isn't there, since its text is the engine's and differs between runs. A `catch (error)` narrows it with `error instanceof Error`, and then reads `error.message` and `error.name`. `TypeError` and the rest wait for library failures to be catchable (below), and subclasses of `Error` for class inheritance.
+JavaScript throws any value. Stage 0 throws nominal Error objects, including subclasses and the standard Error constructors. Errors may be stored, returned from one function and thrown from another. Catch binds an error object as unknown, narrowed by real nominal instanceof tests; it is no longer an unconditional instanceof Error answer. Non-Error throws stay refused because catch does not yet hold every representation.
+
+Built-in errors have a shared name/message/cause prefix and class identity. User fields follow the prefix. Error subclasses use the existing allocator and super initializer, with the dynamic identity fixed before construction. Error.prototype.toString uses JavaScript's empty-name and empty-message rules. Error causes keep a distinct null tag when boxed as unknown, including nullable reference results; null is never represented as undefined. Error.prototype.toString.call(null) throws a nominal, catchable TypeError for its incompatible receiver. stack reads and writes are NotYet: native frames cannot report a JavaScript source stack. Error.toString overrides are NotYet until synthetic built-in overrides are checked for compatible native dispatch. Structural error views are NotYet because the native shape does not represent JavaScript property descriptors.
+
